def parse_response(raw):
    head, _, body = raw.partition(b"\r\n\r\n")
    status_line, *lines = head.decode("iso-8859-1").split("\r\n")
    status = int(status_line.split(" ", 2)[1])
    headers = {}
    for line in lines:
        name, _, value = line.partition(":")
        headers[name.strip().lower()] = value.strip()
    if "content-length" in headers:
        body = body[:int(headers["content-length"])]
    return status, headers, body

def internet_checksum(data):
    if len(data) % 2:
        data = data + b"\x00"
    total = 0
    for i in range(0, len(data), 2):
        total += (data[i] << 8) | data[i + 1]
    while total > 0xFFFF:
        total = (total & 0xFFFF) + (total >> 16)
    return ~total & 0xFFFF

exercises.solution:
```python
def internet_checksum(data):
    if len(data) % 2:
        data = data + b"\x00"
    total = 0
    for i in range(0, len(data), 2):
        total += (data[i] << 8) | data[i + 1]
    while total > 0xFFFF:
        total = (total & 0xFFFF) + (total >> 16)
    return ~total & 0xFFFF
```

Folding the carries back in computes a **one's-complement** sum, in which the arithmetic wraps around from 0xFFFF to 0x0001 rather than to 0. Its useful property is that a header whose checksum field holds the complement of the sum of the other words sums to 0xFFFF in total, so the receiver just checksums everything and checks for 0. It is cheap (a few additions per word, easy to update incrementally when a router changes the TTL) but weak: it cannot detect two swapped 16-bit words, for instance. Link layers add a stronger CRC, and TLS adds cryptographic integrity on top.
